WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.366 --> 00:00:04.458
- There are so many eagles here.

00:00:04.458 --> 00:00:06.042
Bald eagles are our national bird

00:00:06.042 --> 00:00:07.547
and, for most people in the US,

00:00:07.547 --> 00:00:09.548
they're a rare and magnificent sighting.

00:00:09.548 --> 00:00:12.368
But, for us, it's just
part of our daily lives.

00:00:12.368 --> 00:00:15.451
Everybody in town has an eagle story.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.072
I'm Carlin Enlow.

00:00:24.072 --> 00:00:25.745
Born and raised in Unalaska.

00:00:25.745 --> 00:00:29.648
And I am the Unalaska Visitor's
Bureau executive director.

00:00:29.648 --> 00:00:32.174
Even though our island
is almost 80 miles long,

00:00:32.174 --> 00:00:35.596
there are nearly 600 birds
in just a few square miles.

00:00:35.596 --> 00:00:37.224
Which is crazy.

00:00:37.224 --> 00:00:39.218
There's an unnatural amount of birds here

00:00:39.218 --> 00:00:43.385
because we contribute
to their food source.

00:00:43.416 --> 00:00:45.046
These bald eagles are so amazing.

00:00:45.046 --> 00:00:46.795
And we love seeing them.

00:00:46.795 --> 00:00:49.023
But, when they're involved
in your daily life,

00:00:49.023 --> 00:00:53.190
they can make running errands
or your work really difficult.

00:00:54.316 --> 00:00:55.375
- Doing something as simple as

00:00:55.375 --> 00:00:57.573
mailing a letter can be dangerous.

00:00:57.573 --> 00:00:58.549
I'm Officer Wood with the

00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:01.033
Unalaska Department of Public Safety.

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.620
Our department put up these signs

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.882
to warn the community about

00:01:03.882 --> 00:01:06.243
the dangers of the nesting eagles,

00:01:06.243 --> 00:01:10.277
which are right next to the post office.

00:01:10.277 --> 00:01:13.133
Eagles have been known to
swoop down and attack people.

00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:14.313
They're very territorial

00:01:14.313 --> 00:01:16.430
and they're just trying
to protect their nests.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.710
The eagle's nest is right there.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.994
And here is the post office.

00:01:20.994 --> 00:01:23.603
Some people have been hurt
during these incidents.

00:01:23.603 --> 00:01:26.853
But I've also heard some funny stories.

00:01:28.082 --> 00:01:32.249
- I'm Pam Aus and I run Bingo
here at the senior center

00:01:32.363 --> 00:01:34.559
every other Sunday.

00:01:34.559 --> 00:01:36.311
I was carrying this box.

00:01:36.311 --> 00:01:38.469
It had some of my Bingo stuff in it.

00:01:38.469 --> 00:01:40.993
All of a sudden, a wing hit my head.

00:01:40.993 --> 00:01:43.520
The talons came down, grabbed the box,

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:44.740
and flew down the street.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.147
And I never saw my Bingo box again.

00:01:47.147 --> 00:01:49.020
But, if you wanna see
a whole lotta eagles,

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.751
you go to the landfill.

00:01:51.751 --> 00:01:52.769
- I'm Steve Bastick.

00:01:52.769 --> 00:01:56.102
I work at the City of Unalaska Landfill.

00:01:58.928 --> 00:02:00.682
One of my favorite places to be.

00:02:00.682 --> 00:02:03.083
Also, one of the eagles'
favorite places to be.

00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:06.585
I know that eagles are
scavengers and predators.

00:02:06.585 --> 00:02:09.030
So, they like trash, too.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:12.331
They'll pick at whatever
they think might be food.

00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:13.388
- My name is Bob Wilson.

00:02:13.388 --> 00:02:15.915
I've been fishing out of the
Bering Sea and Dutch Harbor

00:02:15.915 --> 00:02:16.892
for 40 years.

00:02:16.892 --> 00:02:18.034
Dutch Harbor is one of the

00:02:18.034 --> 00:02:21.579
largest fishing ports in the world.

00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:24.303
Every time we pull in, we know
the eagles are waiting for us

00:02:24.303 --> 00:02:27.970
so they can steal our
fish as we offload it.

00:02:31.154 --> 00:02:32.256
Do I think they're pests?

00:02:32.256 --> 00:02:33.315
Oh, absolutely.

00:02:33.315 --> 00:02:34.294
But they're cool pests.

00:02:34.294 --> 00:02:35.602
So, that's okay.

00:02:35.602 --> 00:02:37.146
They make a mess of my boat,

00:02:37.146 --> 00:02:39.303
which is kind of the bad part.

00:02:39.303 --> 00:02:42.931
But they are our national
bird and we love them anyway.

00:02:42.931 --> 00:02:43.848
How's that?

